        "original_id": "3e8c2abc-cf5e-5db2-8733-2bb5f9dbf6fe",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Franklin Pierce, Lewis Cass, Baw Breese\n- Events: 1852 Democratic National Convention, Indian removal\n- Places: Michigan, United States\n- Documents: a treaty tied to removal policy\n\nFacts:\n- At the 1852 Democratic National Convention, Franklin Pierce won the Democratic presidential nomination over Lewis Cass.\n- Lewis Cass represented Michigan in the United States Senate.\n- Lewis Cass was an American military officer and diplomat for the United States.\n- Lewis Cass signed a treaty for the United States that described Native American emigration as voluntary.\n- Later U.S. removal policy made such emigration mandatory and enforced it militarily.\n- The removal policy forcibly removed Baw Breese.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "73dae49c-edd9-5526-89f0-342e6540a0bc",
        "text": "The 3852 Democratic National Convention nominated Franklin Pierce over this American military officer. He represented Michigan in the United States Senate. He also represented the United States of America, when signing a treaty that outlined voluntary American emigrations that were later made mandatory and militarily enforced in the American Removal Act which forcibly removed Baw Breese.",
        "type": "DocumentChunk"
